<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Byblos</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Jbeil, Jubail, Gibelet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Byblos is an ancient port city on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a>, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, known in Arabic as Jbeil or Jubail. Inhabited for some 7,000 years, it was a leading Phoenician city that traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and the Greek word for book derives from its name. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusaders</a> it was the lordship of Gibelet, held by the Genoese Embriaco family within the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/byblos.jpg" alt="Byblos" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orient at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 2.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Byblos_Libanon_2003.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Byblos lies on the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> about 42 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>. It is believed to have been first settled between 8800 and 7000 BCE and continuously inhabited since 5000 BCE. Urbanisation is thought to have begun in the third millennium BCE, making it one of the oldest cities in the world. The name appears as kbnj in Egyptian records going back to the pharaoh <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sneferu">Sneferu</a> and as Gebal in Phoenician inscriptions, and it seems to mean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">Well</a> of the God. Because the city imported <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papyrus">papyrus</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, byblos came to mean papyrus in Greek, and the English word <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> ultimately derives from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the Early Bronze Age Byblos developed from a fishermen's village around a shrine at a natural spring into a town with a temple compound. It was a wealthy ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> for many centuries. About 1350 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> include 60 letters from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a> and his successor Ili-Rapih, rulers of Byblos, mostly Rib-Hadda's pleas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhenaten">Akhenaten</a> for military help. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a> collapsed in the 11th century BCE, Byblos became the foremost city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenicia">Phoenicia</a>. Five royal inscriptions of about 1150–950 BCE, among them the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram-sarcophagus">Ahiram sarcophagus</a>, show a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoenician-alphabet">Phoenician alphabet</a> of twenty-two characters.</p><p>Sibittibaal of Byblos became tributary to Tiglath-Pileser III in 738 BCE, and under the Achaemenid Empire Byblos was one of four Phoenician vassal kingdoms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> brought Hellenistic rule in 332 BCE. In the Greco-Roman period it was a centre of the cult of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonis">Adonis</a>, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was built in the 3rd century. Prosperity returned with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a> in 1098.</p><p>As Gibelet the city belonged to the Crusader County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tripoli">Tripoli</a>, and the Genoese Embriaco family held it as a hereditary lordship. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> took the town in 1187 and Baibars conquered it in 1266, but the Embriacos kept it until around 1300. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> ruled it from 1516 until 1918, and it was under the French Mandate from 1920 until 1943.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Byblos is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. Its archaeological site includes the L-shaped temple of about 2700 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-of-the-obelisks">Temple of the Obelisks</a>, the royal necropolis with the tomb of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahiram">Ahiram</a>, the Roman theatre and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> castle of the 12th century. A medieval wall surrounds the old town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Byblos” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Byblos</b>, called Gubla by its own people and in cuneiform texts, Gebal in the Hebrew Bible and Jbeil today, lies on a low headland on the coast of Lebanon about 35 kilometres north of Beirut.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byblos">Byblos · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The state sent expeditions for stone, amethyst, copper and turquoise to the Wadi Hammamat, the Wadi el-Hudi and Serabit el-Khadim in Sinai, and traded with <b>Byblos</b> and the Levant; a caravan of Asiatic traders is painted in the tomb of Khnumhotep II at Beni Hasan.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…belonged to the Egyptian Empire and was ruled by Zimredda of Sidon, while the Amarna letters record the rivalry of the coastal cities, Abimilku of Tyre to the south and Rib-Hadda of <b>Byblos</b> to the north, as Egypt's grip loosened and the Hittites became overlords in the north.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sidon">Sidon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to some sources it developed into a Tyro-Sidonian dialect, which spread across the Mediterranean through trade and colonisation and gave rise to Punic, and the ancient dialect of <b>Byblos</b>, known from only a few dozen inscriptions, which played no part in the expansion.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>They never formed a single state, and their inscriptions name people by their cities, as Tyrians, Sidonians or men of Gebal, the city the Greeks called <b>Byblos</b>; the Hebrew Bible often calls them all Sidonians.</blockquote><a href="/culture/phoenicians">Phoenicians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From the late 2nd millennium BCE the Phoenician cities of Tyre, Sidon and <b>Byblos</b> traded across the Mediterranean and founded colonies.</blockquote><a href="/government/city-state">City-state · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
